opam-version: "2.0"
synopsis: "Torch bindings for OCaml — canary-local, with the dune staging fix"
description: """
CANARY-LOCAL PACKAGE. Byte-for-byte the upstream torch.v0.17.0 opam file
plus two lines: a one-hunk patch to src/wrapper/dune, and avoid-version.

WHY IT EXISTS. torch.v0.17.0 does not build with dune 3.23.1. Its
src/wrapper/dune declares

  (foreign_stubs (language cxx) (names torch_api))

while torch_api.cpp line 944 does `#include "torch_api_generated.cpp"` —
a .cpp included as a header. dune stages the listed .cpp and every .h
(torch_api.h and torch_api_generated.h both land in
_build/default/src/wrapper/) but not the unlisted .cpp, so the compile
dies with

  torch_api.cpp:944:10: fatal error: torch_api_generated.cpp:
                        No such file or directory

The package declares (lang dune 3.11); the staging rule changed under it.
opam's own post-message blames a missing system libtorch, which is a red
herring — libtorch 2.1.2 was installed and its -isystem flags are in the
failing command line. Measured 2026-08-30 on OCaml 5.4.1 / dune 3.23.1;
with the patch, `dune build -p torch` exits 0 with an empty log.

The upstream fix is the patch below, unchanged. This package exists so
canary has a torch that BUILDS, next to the stock package it keeps as a
declared xfail — the finding stays visible as a world rather than being
patched away.

NOT A CHANNEL PAIR. This is the same upstream version, differently
packaged. It does not answer "does today's binding work with tomorrow's
lib"; torch's version axis genuinely has one point (see the binding row's
rationale in canary_project_torch.ml).
"""
maintainer: "weng@cs.jhu.edu"
authors: "Jane Street Group, LLC"
license: "MIT"
homepage: "https://github.com/janestreet/torch"
bug-reports: "https://github.com/janestreet/torch/issues"
dev-repo: "git+https://github.com/janestreet/torch.git"
depends: [
  "ocaml" {>= "5.1.0"}
  "base" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "core" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "core_unix" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "ppx_bench" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "ppx_inline_test" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "ppx_jane" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "ppx_string" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "stdio" {>= "v0.17" & < "v0.18~"}
  "ctypes" {>= "0.18.0"}
  "ctypes-foreign"
  "dune" {>= "3.11.0"}
  "dune-configurator"
  "ocaml-compiler-libs" {>= "v0.11.0"}
  "libtorch" {with-test}
]
depopts: ["libtorch"]
# KEPT VERBATIM from upstream, and it is the point: this is the
# solver-enforced gate (project_pytorch.md §0b). It is why the 2.2.1 lib
# world must place libtorch OUTSIDE opam — `opam install torch
# libtorch.2.2.1` answers "No solution found", measured.
conflicts: [
  "libtorch" {< "2.1.0" | >= "2.2.0"}
]
available: arch != "arm32" & arch != "x86_32"
# the solver must never reach for this package on its own — canary always
# asks for it by explicit version pin
flags: [avoid-version]
patches: ["dune-extra-deps.patch"]
extra-files: [["dune-extra-deps.patch" "md5=a5c1db69fd603914d507e937cfb6b2bd"]]
build: ["dune" "build" "-p" "torch" "-j" jobs]
url {
  src: "https://github.com/janestreet/torch/archive/refs/tags/v0.17.0.tar.gz"
  checksum: [
    "md5=8e189f338b454bbefc4965d827f5a0cc"
    "sha512=f96155624705bc500734d5dbb18255436c1e4646f89ddc0c47260d79003543126c68e206267b3487bdf25c4f7168ab694ba89b661304c4735c159a91630f97cd"
  ]
}
